],[-101.82216,33.62868],[-101.8231,33.62755],[-101.82451,33.62623],[-101.82608,33.62509],[-101.82975,33.62305],[-101.83498,33.62056],[-101.8379,33.61895],[-101.83957,33.61746],[-101.84124,33.61624],[-101.84166,33.61571],[-101.84257,33.61403],[-101.84245,33.61285],[-101.84147,33.61195],[-101.84045,33.61057],[-101.83999,33.60964],[-101.83965,33.60834],[-101.83955,33.60734],[-101.83976,33.60533],[-101.84004,33.60435],[-101.8413,33.60154],[-101.84161,33.60038],[-101.8421,33.5994],[-101.84277,33.5985],[-101.84359,33.59769],[-101.84446,33.59707],[-101.84396,33.59639],[-101.84297,33.59442],[-101.84256,33.593],[-101.84225,33.59086],[-101.84238,33.58881],[-101.84264,33.58665],[-101.8433,33.58457],[-101.84399,33.58318],[-101.84495,33.58189],[-101.84569,33.58051],[-101.84665,33.57921],[-101.84893,33.57672],[-101.85015,33.57553],[-101.85226,33.57411],[-101.85332,33.57267],[-101.85545,33.57065],[-101.85789,33.56892],[-101.86009,33.56779],[-101.86108,33.56709],[-101.86243,33.56637],[-101.86415,33.56549],[-101.86746,33.56425],[-101.86858,33.5639],[-101.87057,33.5635],[-101.87261,33.56337],[-101.87465,33.5635],[-101.87664,33.5639],[-101.87852,33.56455],[-101.88027,33.56544],[-101.88182,33.56654],[-101.88315,33.56784]],"kind":"swath"}],"story":["Two tornadoes came out of the same storm over Lubbock on the night of 11 May 1970. The first was brief. The second grew to a mile and a half across and crossed the heart of the city north-eastward, through the downtown and the neighbourhoods beyond it, after dark.","Twenty-six people were killed and around five hundred injured. The twenty-storey Great Plains Life Building, the tallest in the city, was left standing but damaged through its frame; it still stands.","Ted Fujita surveyed the damage from the air and the ground. A path through a real city \u2014 engineered offices and factories beside frame houses and mobile homes \u2014 let him relate what each kind of building had suffered to the wind that did it, and the scale he published the next year was built on that evidence. Lubbock was among the first tornadoes ever rated F5.","Texas Tech's wind engineering research grew out of the same night, and with it one of the most useful ideas to come out of tornado science: the above-ground safe room."],"facts":[{"label":"Deaths","value":"26"},{"label":"Injured","value":"About 500"},{"label":"Widest","value":"About 1\u00bd miles"},{"label":"Damage","value":"About $135 million","note":"In 1970 dollars"},{"label":"Legacy","value":"The Fujita scale","note":"Published by Fujita in 1971"}]}